  CLI->>GW: instance:create<br/>PROJECT NODE NAME
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit instance:create PROJECT NODE NAME`
  %%> - syntax: `<project> <node> <name> [--root=PATH] [--domain=HOST] [--branch=BRANCH] [--database-server=SLUG] [--wait-active]`
  Note over GW: Validate, reserve Instance<br/>address · T3 port · .env
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the calling Node has node access to the target Node, an active `app-dev` Node
  %%> - a Project with `nested_fleet` on is admitted only as a container in the nested Incus project: `instance.nested_fleet_host_unavailable` (409) when the Node hosts no nested fleets (its switch is off, and it is no CI-only Node whose `ci-runner` converge recorded nested hosting), `instance.nested_fleet_container_required` (409) when the Node is not on the build list or the result is a host checkout, both before any record; the clone is a copy of its default, in the same Incus project
  %%> - the Project's `default` Instance runs in a container on this Node, is active and has a seed snapshot, else `instance.default_missing`; an asleep or stopped default is a valid source: the clone copies the seed snapshot and never wakes it
  %%> - a proof copy may go to another Node that offers proof places when that Node's seed is the default's current seed (the same commit and time taken); it clones that seed, and the database refuses any other clone on another Node than its default
  %%> - Conn is enabled, and the model router has its guard, else `operation.kind_unavailable`
  %%> - the recipe's agent folder pins the program of the Project's `container_server`, else `project.container_server_unavailable` (409) before any record
  %%> - before the admission transaction, the Gateway reads the Project's agent instructions, the `agent/` folder of its Documents (development-containers.md#agent-instructions): at most 32 files and 32 KiB of UTF-8 text (no control character but a tab or a line end) with plain skill names, else `projects.agent_instructions_too_large` or `projects.agent_instructions_invalid` (422) before any record; a store failure refuses with the store's own error
  %%> - a database on a Database server clones on the default's server; another server refuses `instance.database_clone_unsupported`
  %%> - a nested-fleet Project: the Node has the nested-fleet switch, else the placement rule refuses
  %%> - a nested-fleet default whose runtime records an inner volume, while a deployment of it is open: `instance.default_seed_in_flight` (409) before any record, since the deployment retakes the two halves of the seed one after the other
  %%> - a nested-fleet default whose runtime records an inner volume: the clone's runtime records its own `inner-<id>`, and `container.clone` names the default's volume; every other clone, a copy of the first nested-fleet default included, records none
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the Instance is reserved with its runtime record: container name, bridge address, the next free T3 port after the Node's last one, wrapping from 3999 to 3800, the Project's `container_server`, and the default as clone source
  %%> - `.env` is rendered from the Project's env template; it, the placeholder model key and a repository read token go to the vault
  %%> - each attachment of the default marked shared is attached to the clone as it is (same connection, prefix and network, still shared) before `.env` renders, so `.env` holds its keys and the container intent its network path
  %%> - the clone workspace reads one private JSON vault value with `.env`, the Process environment files and the names of the keys the clone owns, of the default's connection keys, of its credential keys and of the shared connections' keys, and the default's Route host with the clone's; SSH stdin takes the whole value
  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent and `container.clone`
  %%> - the Route record and the database records are reserved here; the create creates no database: the completion does
  %%> - the Route publication is captured from the current site catalog: a Route the capture refuses (for example `cluster.router_required`) refuses the create before any record
  %%> Route Nodes:
  %%> - free (no publisher holds a serving Node and none waits for one; for a Caddy-only create fewer than eight shared writers, for any other no shared writer): the create takes them in this transaction and publishes the address; a Caddy-only publication (no PHP pool, DNS record, Ingress site or LAN rule) takes a shared hold, any other takes them alone
  %%> - held: the create never waits; its intent carries no publication, and the completion publishes the address as before
  %%> - the admission records why (`nodes_held`, `share_cap` or `waiter_first`) and logs one info line with it and the shared writers on each Node
  %%> - the decision is one insert per Node into the hold tables in one transaction: Caddy-only creates admitted together all publish; of two other creates admitted together, exactly one takes the Nodes
  %%> - a retained create retry records the Project's current development Processes in this admission transaction; changed definitions update their records, removed definitions remove theirs
  %%> - each Process takes its desired state from the default's copy of the same definition: stopped on the default stays stopped (installed, not started, startable by `process:start`); failed, absent or running runs
  Note over GW: Commit, push copies<br/>queue start · answer 202
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID
  GW->>SW: Start run from queue<br/>operation-instance-create
  opt a Route, its Nodes taken at admission
    SW->>N: Publish the address<br/>clone-route-csr · clone-route
    %%> When:
    %%> - in its own job, `publication`, at the run's first level, beside the copy; no clone step waits for it
    %%> - `clone-route-input` writes the run folder and the playbooks that the site child reads, beside the CSR: a clone runs no `input` step
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - per leaf, the key and CSR on the Node and the signature (`clone-route-csr-<i>`); then the Route create children (`clone-route`): the site in the Node's Caddy, the private DNS, the Ingress edge of a public Route and the TLS probe
    %%> - the site puts the Route's own Caddy groups into what the Node holds, under the Node lock; every other block, its order and the global options stay as the Node holds them (docs/reference/routes.md#publish-a-development-site); its Node steps take one of 16 names per Node (`route-site-<node>-<route id % 16>`)
    %%> - the site step skips the Route access and `APP_URL` (`prepared` names this Node): the copy's root disk does not exist yet, and `.env` comes with the checkout
    %%> - Caddy proxies to the bridge address reserved at admission; until the completion starts the units, the address answers TLS and then 502
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - both steps allow their failure, so the run and the create still succeed; `result.route_publication` is `failed`, the Route records `instance.create_route_failed`, and the completion publishes it again
    %%> - a merge that a Node refuses changes no file there and marks the run folder: `route_publication` is `deferred`, no Route warning, and the completion publishes the address
    SW-->>GW: clone-route ended<br/>native progress event
    Note over GW: Route active, free Nodes<br/>one transaction
    %%> When:
    %%> - the run's progress event of `clone-route` succeeded; no step calls the Gateway
    %%> In one transaction:
    %%> - the Route turns `active` with its sites published, so the next Route writer's render keeps this site
    %%> - the create's Route Nodes are freed, and waiting Route writes start oldest first
    %%> Otherwise:
    %%> - a failed or skipped `clone-route`, or a lost event, keeps the Nodes until the create's terminal state frees them
    %%> - a waiting Route write that rendered its whole Caddyfile at its admission is refused at its start with `route.publication_stale`, because this site changed what its Nodes serve; a waiting create renders again and a removal composes from the live file
    %%> - a Route that another write took meanwhile, such as a retiring one, stays as it is
  end
  opt a proof copy
    SW->>N: Hold the CI place<br/>native clone-ci-hold
    %%> When:
    %%> - first in the clone job, before the copy exists; every other clone skips it
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - first checks that the `ci-runner` role's hold script is on the Node. When it is missing, the step calls nothing and prints a refusal; the copy does not start, and the create fails with `instance.ci_hold_unavailable`, naming the Node
    %%> - runs the `ci-runner` role's hold script as root: `orbit-ci-runner-hold hold <copy> <places>`, with the Node's `ci_places`
    %%> - records the copy's hold, then stops idle runner services on the Node down to the places left to runners; a busy runner is never stopped (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#proof-places-on-a-ci-node)
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - the copy does not start; the copy's destroy releases the hold
  end
    SW->>N: Clone default container<br/>native clone-container
    %%> Stage, the step's first part:
    %%> - SSH stdin is the Gateway's launch copy, one vault value on ONE line: `stage` (the clone script, its helpers and the container intent from the admitting Gateway's release) beside `private` (the read token's credential line and the model environment). Swamp turns a vault value with a newline into one regular expression, which a 32 KB line breaks (release 2080)
    %%> - Swamp masks the launch copy only as a whole; the clone script's own redaction (each stdin line and value, credentials by shape) keeps a lone token or model key out of a failure detail
    %%> - the launcher (the intent's `clone_launcher`) writes `stage` into a private folder for this run and gives the clone script only `private`; later steps run the staged launcher by path
    %%> - scripts and intent never fill the SSH command; a copy that cannot be staged fails the step with `instance.clone_input_failed`
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the copy supplies the full root disk device (type, path, pool and size), whether the seed inherits it or stores it locally
    %%> - `incus copy` of the default container's snapshot `seed-<commit>`, a ZFS clone; a stopped default needs no start
    %%> Inner volume (a clone whose intent has `inner_volume` only; every other clone makes no volume call and copies the container as before):
    %%> - the default's volume must hold its own snapshot `seed-<commit>`, the other half of the seed, else `instance.default_seed_incomplete` before any change; a deployment of the default takes the pair
    %%> - before the container: `incus storage volume copy` of that snapshot to `inner-<id>`, a ZFS clone; Incus gives the copy a new btrfs fsid (about 4 s in the 2026-11-14 test); a volume of that name without its container, left by a failed copy, is deleted and copied again. The clone inherits `volmode=dev` from the pool's `custom` dataset, which the Node's host converge sets, so its device is made once (docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-inner-volume)
    %%> - then `incus storage volume set` writes the intent's `inner_volume.size` on `inner-<id>`: a seed snapshot that a seed copy moved to a CI Node records `size: 0`, and the copy takes the snapshot's config; the ZFS volume keeps its size, so only the record changes
    %%> - the container copy sets the disk `inner-pool` to `source=inner-<id>`, so the copy never attaches the default's volume
    %%> - a failed container copy deletes the volume it copied, so a failed create leaves no volume without its container
    %%> - the copy gets its own bridge address and ACL, and the proxy device `t3`: NAT from the Node's WireGuard address and the T3 port to T3's port 3773
    %%> - the container's ACL lets the Node's Caddy reach port 8080, WireGuard peers reach port 3773, and the container reach its database server's port
    %%> - UFW: a route rule admits WireGuard peers to T3, below a deny for the task VM range; a rule admits the container to its database server's port
    %%> - before the first start, the stopped copy gets the Orbit root in its CA bundle when the seed's bundle lacks it, so Chromium trusts Orbit's sites; a copy without a bundle refuses
    %%> - before the first start, the stopped copy gets the composed `NO_PROXY` drop-ins, the bytes `env:sync` writes
    %%> - before the first start, the stopped copy gets T3's files and last `/etc/orbit/instance`, so its boot starts T3; a drop-in turns off T3's default dependencies, so T3 waits for nftables, the local file systems, the journal and sysctl, not for `sysinit.target`; a second drop-in sets `ExecStart` to the program of the runtime's recorded container server, `bin/t3` or `bin/conn-server`, which every later call in the container runs too
    %%> - before the first start, the stopped copy gets `/etc/machine-info` with `PRETTY_HOSTNAME=<instance>.<project slug>`, so T3's first start shows that name; the copy's Incus templates leave the file alone
    %%> - at the first start of a copy of an older seed, its app user and group get their new names before any other command in it, as at a start: `orbit` (uid 1000) becomes `web`, the group `work` (gid 2760) becomes `web`, and the group 1000 goes (docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-app-users-name)
    %%> - a nested-fleet copy starts only through the start guard: its config and devices exactly as the intent writes them, no `inner-pool` device without an inner volume in the intent, the copy's inner volume with exactly its keys, and a root volume without `zfs.delegate`
    %%> - right after the start, after the copy's own templates rewrote `/etc/hosts` and `/etc/hostname`: one exec writes `/etc/hostname`, sets the kernel hostname and reads `/etc/hosts`; the clone then writes the plan's private names block with the renderer `env:sync` uses and the `127.0.1.1` line of the machine name, each keeping the other's lines, only when the file changes; T3 has already started with the boot by then, and its label came from the `/etc/machine-info` boot file; a failure of any of it is `instance.clone_private_names_failed`
    %%> Mail:
    %%> - the same launch applies the exact SMTP path before the four root-owned mail units; no separate mail playbook or step
    %%> - two IPv4 loopback sockets forward 1025 and 2525; foreign units or listeners skip setup without stopping their Process
    %%> - the seed's mail socket links stay: the units are the Node's, so a clone's launch reports `unchanged` unless the Node's endpoint changed
    %%> On mail failure:
    %%> - keep a mail warning and continue workspace, T3 and registration; mail:update remains the repair command
    %%> ACL:
    %%> - reads the copy's own ACL by its name, never the list of every ACL on the Node; a missing ACL is created, a different one is set
    %%> Branch (a branch the seed already holds needs no network and no mirror):
    %%> - an explicit branch choice (`branch_selection`) runs no `ls-remote`: the admission read GitHub. A new branch whose admitted source commit is the seed commit fetches nothing; any other choice fetches its source into the Node's mirror with the read token
    %%> - a create by name only reads GitHub with `ls-remote`: a branch on the origin is fetched into the Node's mirror; a branch the origin lacks fetches nothing
    %%> - the Node's mirror holds the branch only when it was fetched; the token stays on the Node
    %%> - a branch made on GitHub after the admission: the copy still starts at the admitted commit; a later push of it is refused as `tasks.not_fast_forward` when the GitHub branch has commits the copy lacks, and fast-forwards, losing no commit, when it does not
    %%> Timing:
    %%> - each clone phase prints the seconds of each of its parts; the result carries them as `timings`, so every create shows where its time went
    opt nested-fleet clone with a Club key
      SW->>C: Deliver the Club key<br/>native clone-club-key
      %%> When: right after the copy's first start; the copy's inner Swamp serve starts with the container and reads the key from `/run/orbit-club`
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - SSH stdin into the copy's /run (a tmpfs, never a snapshot); a new key restarts the nested Gateway's Swamp serve
      %%> - the result's `nested_fleet_club_key` is what this step did; a clone that skips it says `missing`
      %%> - the inner fleet starts later, in the completion ([instance:complete](docs/cli/instance/complete.mmd))
      %%> On failure:
      %%> - `instance.nested_club_key_not_tmpfs` or `instance.nested_club_key_delivery_failed`; the checkout and T3 do not run
    end
    SW->>C: Check out and wait for T3<br/>native clone-t3
    %%> When: after the copy and the Club key; a failed copy or Club key skips it
    %%> Why one step: the checkout, T3 and the sessions check were three steps on two levels, each with about 1 s of Swamp start; one SSH call runs them, the checkout beside T3
    %%> Why beside: the T3 part never writes the workspace; T3 runs Git with `GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0`, so it takes no Git lock
    %%> SSH stdin: the workspace's private JSON; the model environment is already in the copy, written by the copy step before the first start
    %%> Checkout, beside the agent part and T3:
    %%> - a branch in the Node's mirror goes in as a bundle; a branch the mirror lacks starts at the seed commit, which the seed's own repository holds, with no bundle; it is checked out in `/srv/workspace`; the group, setgid bits and default ACLs come with the clone
    %%> - runs only the guest's `checkout` part: the bundle, the checkout mark, `.env`; PHP settings, Process files and units are the completion's `services` part
    %%> - after the checkout it checks the branch and commit, then writes the root-owned mark `/var/lib/orbit/workspace-checkout` with the Instance id and commit; when the mark already names this Instance, a retry skips the fetch and checkout, so the branch never moves and the result's commit stays the mark's
    %%> - writes `.env` for `web` with group `web`: the default's file from the seed, with the clone's own connection keys, the keys of the connections it shares with its default and `APP_URL`, without the default's other connection and Reverb keys or a copy of its credentials; `APP_KEY` stays the default's, because the cloned data is encrypted with it
    %%> - `.env` names the clone's database before the completion creates it; nothing in the container reads it before the completion starts the units
    %%> - a copied value that points at the default's Route host (its Route without a web root) points at the clone's Route host; the rest of the value stays
    %%> - prints only the names of the copied, dropped, rewritten and shared keys; the result carries them as `environment`
    %%> Task copy (`container.clone.clean_workspace`), at the first checkout, before the mark:
    %%> - excludes `/.env`, clears index flags, then `git reset --hard` and, after it, `git clean -d -ff` without `-x` until a dry run finds nothing new, with the task check's ignore rules: the default's modified and untracked files go, what the start commit's ignore rules name stays
    %%> - `git status --porcelain` must be empty, else the step fails
    %%> - the result carries the counts and the first 20 names of the reset and removed paths as `workspace_cleaned`; a clone without the mark keeps the default's tree
    %%> Agent part, then T3:
    %%> - the agent part finds the model environment in place and writes the Claude Code and Codex settings that point at the model forwarder; a copy without the model environment fails the part
    %%> - makes the agent folder `/home/agent/.config/orbit/agent` again from `clone.agent_instructions`, which only this part's intent carries, owned by root and readable by `agent`: `AGENTS.md` (the note of the Project type, then the Project's own text) and the Project's `.skills`; a path out of the folder, or a folder reached through a link, fails the part: `instance.create_agent_failed`
    %%> - links `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` and `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` of `agent` to the folder's `AGENTS.md`, and `~/.claude/skills` and `~/.agents/skills` to its `.skills`, and writes `/etc/orbit/instance`, owned by root and readable by all; files the copy step wrote are found in place
    %%> - the T3 part: a T3 that the copy's boot started is not started again (`timings.t3.at_boot` 1); a T3 that does not run is started here, and one that failed at boot is started once more after `reset-failed`, named `restart:t3`; T3 creates its identity at its first start
    %%> - while T3 starts or runs, T3's own CLI issues the `Orbit Gateway` and `Orbit Swamp` sessions at once, as `agent`; the tokens go only to the run's private folder
    %%> - after T3 answers, the T3 project `/srv/workspace` is added as `agent`; during T3's start that call fails
    %%> - waits up to 30 s for `/.well-known/t3/environment`, else `instance.t3_unready`
    %%> Sessions check, after both:
    %%> - issues no session: it checks the two that the T3 part issued against the running T3, and the branch and the checkout mark
    %%> - checks no Process unit: the units start in the completion, whose check refuses a dead one with `instance.container_process_inactive`
    %%> - the checked reply replaces the issued sessions in the private `t3.json`; the step prints the checkout's lines and T3's receipt, with when T3 answered and when the sessions passed
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - before each part the step writes the part's name to its private `t3.json`; the checkout's name is written only when the checkout failed and the T3 part did not; `clone-session-copy` brings that file beside the run folder
    %%> - the result's `failed_step` follows the part: `workspace` with `instance.create_workspace_failed`, `t3` with `instance.create_agent_failed` or `instance.t3_unready`, `register` with `instance.create_register_failed` for the sessions check
    %%> - the Operation's failure detail is the part's own line, such as `instance.container_checkout_failed` or `instance.container_t3_failed`, or the timeout
    SW->>N: Copy the checked sessions<br/>native clone-session-copy
    %%> What it does: brings the checked reply from the Node's private folder to `<run folder>-t3.json` on 209; after a failure, the failed part's name. It is the clone job's last step
    SW->>C: Register the T3 server<br/>native clone-register
    %%> When: the first step of the create job, after the clone steps and the publication, beside the private folder's removal
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - see conn/environment-renew.mmd: the step checks the environment id and scopes, and the sessions go to the vault as sensitive fields
    %%> - removes the copied `t3.json` on every path, before any check can refuse
    %%> - builds the create's result from the same inputs as `finish` (one map), with only the sessions' vault keys; a ready result removes the run folder, so `finish` and the cleanup step skip
    %%> - a refused check fails the step: `finish` then builds the failed result with `failed_step: register`
  alt all steps pass in budget
    C-->>SW: T3 answers
    %%> When:
    %%> - `clone-register` starts when the clone steps and the publication have both ended; at the measured times the publication ends first
    %%> - its ready result is the run's result (the Gateway reads `clone-register` when `finish` skipped); `finish`, the stop and the cleanup step skip, and the run ends with the `accepted` assert, which costs no dispatch
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    Note over GW: Read baseline, record T3<br/>admit instance.complete
    %%> Before the transaction:
    %%> - reads the workspace baseline over SSH (`ContainerWorkspaceRead::baseline`, at most 4 s) and the T3 session from the vault; no lock waits on the network
    %%> In one transaction:
    %%> - the clone stays `source_resolved` with its `starting_commit`; the runtime is `running`; the Conn layer records the T3 server from the result
    %%> - writes `workspace_baseline` and `workspace_baseline_at`, then turns the create `succeeded`, so the baseline is older than the create's end and than any pairing
    %%> - after the create row is terminal, admits `instance.complete` with the Instance lock, so nothing takes the Instance in between; the result's `completion_operation_id` names it
    %%> - the completion's vault copy and its Swamp start run after the commit
    %%> - a completion that is refused all the same (the Route capture again, after a change to the fleet) is recorded as a failed `instance.complete` with that code, `failed_step: complete` and the recovery `instance:complete`; another error is recorded as `operation.admission_failed`; the create still succeeds and names it
    %%> - a `route_publication` of `published` turns the Route `active` here too, when its progress event was lost; `failed` marks the Route `failed_step: native-create`, `instance.create_route_failed`
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the user can pair with T3 and work; the Processes, the database and a nested fleet follow in the completion
    %%> - `route_publication`: `published` (the address answers TLS), `deferred` (the Route Nodes were held at admission, or a Node refused the merge of the site: the completion publishes) or `failed` (the completion publishes again); absent without a Route
    %%> - `route_publication_reason` beside `deferred` only: `nodes_held`, `share_cap`, `waiter_first` (at admission) or `node_refused` (in `clone-route`); the API result carries it; Conn and the web views show nothing new, and the CLI prints it only inside the whole result (`--json`, `operation:show`)
    %%> - "fully set up" is only the Instance's `status: active`, which a succeeded completion sets ([instance:complete](docs/cli/instance/complete.mmd))
    %%> - the result's `processes_stopped` names the Processes the completion installs but leaves stopped, as on the default
    %%> - the receipt includes when T3 answered and when its sessions passed
    %%> On a failed baseline read:
    %%> - the create fails with `instance.workspace_baseline_unavailable`, `failed_step: register`; the T3 server is not recorded and no completion starts
    %%> - the container keeps running; a retry of the create runs again over it, and `instance:destroy` removes it
  else Instance already created
    Note over GW: Skipped — already created<br/>instance.exists
  else a step fails or the budget ends
    C--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout
    Note over SW: finish: the failed result<br/>stop-input: the stop's run folder
    SW->>N: Attempt container stop<br/>incus stop
    %%> What stays:
    %%> - the reservation, runtime, and any container, workspace and owned database records stay until the owner acts; the create made no database
    %%> - a published address stays `active` (502 until a retry or `instance:destroy`); a publication that started and did not succeed is withdrawn by `withdraw-route`, under the Nodes the create still holds
    %%> - a refused retry removes none of them; stop is attempted; failed compensation leaves its step failed
    %%> - shared service endpoint records and rules may remain even after destroy until endpoint lifecycle work is live
    %%> - failed and cancelled creates, including cancellation before work starts, report retained_for_retry, the same create command with a new idempotency key, and forced destroy
    %%> Cleanup:
    %%> - remove the private clone folder after success or failure, beside `clone-register`, even if staging or the first launch failed
    %%> - the cleanup step removes the run folder and the copied `t3.json` after a failure
    %%> - a failed `stop-input` leaves the stop without its playbooks: the container keeps running until a retry replaces it or `instance:destroy` removes it
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step, error_code
    Note over GW: Record the failure
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again; it resumes on the same container
    %%> - a resume never moves a branch this Instance checked out: commits on it stay
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
  end
  opt --wait-active
    loop every 2 s until terminal
      CLI->>GW: operation:show COMPLETION
      GW-->>CLI: status + result
    end
    CLI->>GW: instance:show INSTANCE
    GW-->>CLI: status
    %%> - exits 0 only when the Instance is `active`; any other end exits 1
  end
